proof_steps:
Step 1. Form the difference quotient of $h = f/g$ and find a common denominator. Since $g$ is differentiable at $c$, it is continuous at $c$, so $\lim_{x \to c} g(x) = g(c) \neq 0$. By the non-vanishing limit theorem, $g(x) \neq 0$ for all $x$ in some deleted neighbourhood of $c$, ensuring $h$ is defined near $c$. For $x \neq c$ with $g(x) \neq 0$:
\[
  \frac{h(x) - h(c)}{x - c}
  = \frac{\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} - \frac{f(c)}{g(c)}}{x - c}
  = \frac{f(x)g(c) - f(c)g(x)}{g(x)g(c)(x - c)}.
\]

